Generates a C++ header file containing golden test vectors: for each sampled stroke sequence from the infinite dictionary, it renders the reference walker's output across all three profiles (plain, smart, nvim) and its validity, which the javelin-ext C++ unit test replays to verify agreement between the generated dictionary and the Rust walker.

The output is pure test input — never consulted by lookup — so answer strings here don't compromise the data header's integrity. Each sequence is converted to a uint32_t stroke-mask array and a GenGolden struct row recording the walker's verdict via `render_walk`. An unmatched sequence is marked invalid with empty values; a non-terminal match (partial progress) is valid and carries its rendered values so firmware displays the same "in progress" feedback as enumerated paths.

`emit_test_header(types, constructs) -> Result<String>` orchestrates the generation: calls `sample_sequences` to get test cases, builds mask arrays and golden rows via `stroke_mask` and `walk`, then wraps them in a fixed C++ preamble and namespace closure. `walk(seq, types, constructs)` resolves a sequence against the trie; `render_walk(&result, Profile)` stringifies each profile's output; `c_escape(s)` handles string literals. All three depend on the sampled sequences and trie structure staying in sync — if sampling or the walk semantics change, test vectors must regenerate or C++ assertions will fail.